"Arithmetic and Geometry of Markov polynomials (https://armj.math.stonybrook.edu/Articles/250708-Winn/index.html)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://armj.math.stonybrook.edu/Articles/250708-Winn/index.html\",\"pattern\":\"Conjecture 6.3\"}); Total lines: 981\nL427: (24)  |  | $${R}_{2k+1}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{𝒟}}\\left(({\\lambda }_{+}-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}){\\lambda }_{+}^{k}-({\\lambda }_{-}-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}){\\lambda }_{-}^{k}\\right).$$  |\nL428: ## cite9†Image 6 Log-Concavity cite10†Image L429: \nL430: A large number of naturally-occurring combinatorial sequences exhibit the logarithmically concave (log-concave) property [31]. Further to saturation, experimental data that we have looked-at so far has shown that coefficients of the Markov polynomials are log-concave in all directions.\nL431: ###### Definition 6.1. cite10†Image L432: \nL433: A sequence $x=({x}_{0},{x}_{1},\\mathrm{\\dots },{x}_{n})$ is said to be log-concave if it satisfies the property\nL434: \nL435: (25)  |  | $${x}_{k}^{2}\\ge {x}_{k-1}{x}_{k+1},$$  |\nL436: \nL437: for $k\\in \\{1,2,\\mathrm{\\dots },n-1\\}$.\nL438: \nL439: A well-known example is the binomial sequence ${x}_{k}=\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{k}\\right),k=0,\\mathrm{\\dots },n$:\nL440:  | $$\\frac{{x}_{k}^{2}}{{x}_{k-1}{x}_{k+1}}={\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{k}\\right)}^{2}/\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{k-1}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{k+1}\\right)=\\frac{k+1}{k}\\frac{n-k+1}{n-k}>1.$$  |\nL441: ###### Definition 6.2. cite10†Image L442: \nL443: We say that the weights on the Newton polygon satisfy the weak log-concavity property if their sequences in all principal directions (horizontal, vertical and diagonal with $i+j=\\text{constant}$) are log-concave.\nL444: ###### Conjecture 6.3. cite10†Image L445: \nL446: (Log-concavity conjecture) The coefficients of Markov polynomials are weakly log-concave on the corresponding Newton polygon.\nL447: \nL448: Note that the Conjecture 6.3 implies the Saturation Conjecture 3.3, claiming the positivity of all the coefficients inside the Newton polygon. Indeed, we know that the coefficients on the boundary are positive, so the appearance of internal zero coefficients will contradict the log-concavity property (25).\nL449: ###### Theorem 6.4. cite10†Image L450: \nL451: Conjecture 6.3 holds for the Markov-Fibonacci polynomials.\nL452: ###### Proof.\nL453: \nL454: We need to check the log-concavity condition in all three principal directions. Using (16), in the horizontal direction we have\nL455:  | $${A}_{i,j}^{2}={\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n-j}{n+1-i-j}\\right)}^{2}{\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{i+j}{j}\\right)}^{2}=\\left(\\frac{{(n-j)!}^{2}}{{(n+1-i-j)!}^{2}{(i-1)!}^{2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{{(i+j)!}^{2}}{{i!}^{2}{j!}^{2}}\\right),$$  |\nL456:  | ${A}_{i-1,j}{A}_{i+1,j}$  | $=\\left({\\displaystyle \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n-j}{n+2-i-j}}\\right)\\left({\\displaystyle \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n-j}{n-i-j}}\\right)\\left({\\displaystyle \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{i+j-1}{j}}\\right)\\left({\\displaystyle \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{i+j+1}{j}}\\right)$  |\nL457:  |  | $=\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{{(n-j)!}^{2}}{(n+2-i-j)!(i-2)!(n-i-j)!i!}}\\right)\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{(i-1+j)!(i+1+j)!}{{j!}^{2}(i-1)!(i+1)!}}\\right).$  |\nL458: This implies that\nL459: \nL460:  | ${\\displaystyle \\frac{{A}_{i,j}^{2}}{{A}_{i-1,j}{A}_{i+1,j}}}$  | $=\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{n+2-i-j}{n+1-i-j}}\\right)\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{i}{i-1}}\\right)\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{i+j}{i+j+1}}\\right)\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{i+1}{i}}\\right)$  |\nL461:  |  | $=\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{n+2-i-j}{n+1-i-j}}\\right)\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{i}{i-1}}\\right)\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{i(i+1)+j(i+1)}{i(i+1)+ji}}\\right).$  |\nL462: We see that all three terms here are greater than $1$ and so we have the required inequality ${A}_{i,j}^{2}>{A}_{i-1,j}{A}_{i+1,j}.$\nL463: \nL464: Similarly in the vertical case we have\nL465: \nL466:  | $$\\frac{{A}_{i,j}^{2}}{{A}_{i,j-1}{A}_{i,j+1}}=\\left[\\left(\\frac{n-j}{n+1-j}\\right)\\left(\\frac{n+2-i-j}{n+1-i-j}\\right)\\right]\\left[\\left(\\frac{i+j}{i+j+1}\\right)\\left(\\frac{j+1}{j}\\right)\\right]$$  |\nL467:  | $$=\\left[\\frac{(n+1-i-j)(n-j)+(n-j)}{(n+1-i-j)(n-j)+(n+1-i-j)}\\right]\\left[\\frac{j(i+j)+(i+j)}{j(i+j)+j}\\right],$$  |\nL468: with both of the final fractions being greater than $1$ (the first requires that $i\\ge 1$ but the vertical line for $i=0$ consists of only a single point, so can be ignored).\nL469: \nL470: In the diagonal case the second binomials in ${A}_{i-1,j+1},{A}_{i,j},{A}_{i+1,j-1}$ are respectively $\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{i+j}{j+1}\\right),\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{i+j}{j}\\right),\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{i+j}{j-1}\\right),$ which are consecutive binomial coefficients satisfying the log-concavity property. As a result we have\nL471:  | $$\\frac{{A}_{i,j}^{2}}{{A}_{i-1,j+1}{A}_{i+1,j-1}}\\ge \\left(\\frac{{(n-j)!}^{2}}{(n-j+1)!(n-j-1)!}\\right)\\left(\\frac{(n-i-j)!(n+2-i-j)!}{{(n+1-i-j)!}^{2}}\\right)$$  |\nL472:  | $$=\\left(\\frac{n-j}{n-j+1}\\right)\\left(\\frac{n+2-i-j}{n+1-i-j}\\right)=\\frac{(n-j)(n+1-i-j)+(n-j)}{(n-j)(n+1-i-j)+(n+1-i-j)},$$  |\nL473: \nL474: which is greater than or equal to $1$ as required. ∎\nL475: The Conjecture 6.3 holds along the boundaries of the polygon, since these coefficients are precisely binomial coefficients, which are known to be log-concave. We will show now that this holds also on the $2$nd horizontal line and upper diagonal of the Newton polygon.\nL476: ###### Lemma 6.5. cite10†Image L477: \nL478: Sequences of the form\nL479: \nL480:  | $${x}_{k}=A\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k}\\right)+B\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-1}\\right),k=0,1,\\mathrm{\\dots },c$$  |\nL481: \nL482: for positive $A,B,c$ are always log-concave.\nL483: ###### Proof.\nL484: \nL485: We have\nL486:  | $${x}_{k}^{2}={A}^{2}{\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k}\\right)}^{2}+2AB\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-1}\\right)+{B}^{2}{\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-1}\\right)}^{2},$$  |\nL487:  | $$\\begin{array}{c}{x}_{k-1}{x}_{k+1}={A}^{2}\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-1}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k+1}\\right)+AB\\left[\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-1}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k}\\right)+\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-2}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k+1}\\right)\\right]\\hfill \\\\ \\hfill +{B}^{2}\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-2}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k}\\right).\\end{array}$$  |\nL488: It is clear that the coefficients of ${A}^{2}$ and ${B}^{2}$ are greater in the case of ${x}_{k}^{2}$ due to the log-concavity of the binomial coefficients. So if we can show that\nL489: \nL490:  | $$\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-1}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k}\\right)\\ge \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-2}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k+1}\\right),$$  |\nL491: \nL492: then the result will follow. Since\nL493:  | $$\\frac{\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-1}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k}\\right)}{\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-2}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k+1}\\right)}=\\frac{k+1}{k-1}\\frac{c-k+2}{c-k}>1,$$  |\nL494: \nL495: this completes the proof. ∎\nL496: ###### Theorem 6.6. cite10†Image L497: \nL498: The coefficients of Markov polynomial ${M}_{\\rho }$ that appear on the lines with $j=1$ and $i+j=a+b-2$ of its Newton polygon are strictly log-concave.\nL499: \nL500: ###### Proof.\nL501: \nL502: The proof follows from Theorem 4.2 and the previous lemma. ∎\nL503: \nL504: As for the $3$rd diagonal we are only able to prove the log-concavity in the case where $\\rho \\le 3/5$.\nL505: ###### Theorem 6.7. cite10†Image L506: \nL507: The coefficients of Markov polynomials ${M}_{\\rho }$ that appear on the line with $i+j=a+b-3$ of its Newton polygon are log-concave for $\\frac{a}{b}\\le \\frac{3}{5}$.\nL508: \nL509: ###### Proof.\nL510: \nL511: We will use the following result quoted by Stanley [31] (but which dates back to Newton [25]).\nL512: ###### Theorem 6.8 (Stanley [31]). cite10†Image L513: \nL514: Let $p(X)={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n}{\\alpha }_{k}{X}^{k}$. If the polynomial $p(X)$ has only real zeros, then the sequence ${\\alpha }_{k}$ is log-concave.\nL515: \nL516: Let ${\\alpha }_{k}$ be the coefficients on the diagonal $i+j-3$ stated in Theorem 4.2:\nL517:  | $${\\alpha }_{k}=E\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{a+b-3}{k}\\right)+F\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{a+b-4}{k-1}\\right)+G\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{a+b-5}{k-2}\\right),$$  |\nL518:  | $$E=\\frac{(a-1)(a-2)}{2},F=a(b-a-1),G=\\frac{1}{2}\\left[{(b-a)}^{2}+5a-3b\\right].$$  |\nL519: \nL520: Let\nL521: \nL522:  | $$p(X)=\\sum _{k=0}^{a+b-3}{\\alpha }_{k}{X}^{k},$$  |\nL523: \nL524: then we have\nL525: \nL526:  | $p(X)$  | $=E{(1+X)}^{a+b-3}+FX{(1+X)}^{a+b-4}+G{X}^{2}{(1+X)}^{a+b-5}$  |\nL527:  |  | $={(1+X)}^{a+b-5}\\left[E{(1+X)}^{2}+FX(1+X)+G{X}^{2}\\right]={(1+X)}^{a+b-5}q(X),$  |\nL528: \nL529: where\nL901:   * [4] Ph. Caldero, A.V. Zelevinsky Laurent expansions in cluster algebras via quiver representations. Moscow Mathematical Journal, 2006, 6:3 (2006), 411–429.\nL902:   * [5] İ. Çanakçı, R. Schiffler Snake graphs and continued fractions. European J. of Combinatorics 86 (2020), article 103081.\nL903:   * [6] H. Cohn Approach to Markoff’s minimal forms through modular functions. Annals of Math. 61 (1955), 1–12.\nL904:   * [7] J.H. Conway The Sensual (Quadratic) Form, Carus Mathematical Monographs, Vol.26. Mathematical Association of America, 1997.\nL931:   * × M. Aigner Markov’s Theorem and 100 Years of the Uniqueness Conjecture: A Mathematical Journey from Irrational Numbers to Perfect Matchings. Springer, 2013.\nL932:   * × V.I. Arnold Continued fractions. Moscow Center of Continuous Mathematical Education, Moscow, 2002 (in Russian).\nL933:   * × J. Bourgain, A. Gamburd and P. Sarnak. Markoff surfaces and strong approximation. Comptes Rendus de l’Acad. Sci. 354:2 (2016), 131–135.\nL934:   * × Ph. Caldero, A.V. Zelevinsky Laurent expansions in cluster algebras via quiver representations. Moscow Mathematical Journal, 2006, 6:3 (2006), 411–429.\nL935:   * × İ. Çanakçı, R. Schiffler Snake graphs and continued fractions. European J. of Combinatorics 86 (2020), article 103081.\nL936:   * × H. Cohn Approach to Markoff’s minimal forms through modular functions. Annals of Math. 61 (1955), 1–12.\nL937:   * × J.H. Conway The Sensual (Quadratic) Form, Carus Mathematical Monographs, Vol.26. Mathematical Association of America, 1997.\nL938:   * × J. Eddy, E. Fuchs, M. Litman, D. Martin, N. Tripeny Connectivity of Markoff mod-$p$ graphs and maximal divisors. Proc. London Math. Soc. 130 (2025) article e70027.\nL939:   * × S.J. Evans Quantum Numbers and Markov Polynomials. PhD Thesis, Loughborough University, September 2025.\n"
